# Sandbox credentials for the integration suite.
#
# Copy to .env and fill in. .env is gitignored — never commit real keys, and
# never put a production key here: the suite refuses to run unless the server
# key starts with SB-Mid-server-.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#   composer test:sandbox

# Dashboard > Settings > Access Keys, with Environment set to Sandbox.
MIDTRANS_SANDBOX_SERVER_KEY=SB-Mid-server-
MIDTRANS_SANDBOX_CLIENT_KEY=SB-Mid-client-

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Webhook testing through a tunnel
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Midtrans has to reach your machine to deliver a notification. Rather than
# setting a Notification URL in the dashboard, point it per-transaction with the
# X-Override-Notification header — that is what this becomes. Nothing in the
# dashboard changes, and two developers can test at the same time.
#
#   vendor/bin/testbench serve
#   cloudflared tunnel --url http://127.0.0.1:8000
#
# then paste the https://....trycloudflare.com URL here, with the webhook path:
MIDTRANS_OVERRIDE_NOTIFICATION_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Snap-BI — optional, leave the whole block commented out unless you use it
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Snap-BI is a separate product with its own onboarding. If you only use Core
# API and Snap (charge, VA, QRIS, GoPay, webhooks), none of this is needed and
# the package works without it.
#
# Getting credentials, in order:
#
#   1. Generate your keypair — RSA 2048 minimum, PKCS#8, PEM encoded:
#
#        openssl genpkey -algorithm rsa -out secrets/snapbi-sandbox-private.pem \
#            -outform PEM -pkeyopt rsa_keygen_bits:2048
#        openssl rsa -in secrets/snapbi-sandbox-private.pem -outform PEM \
#            -pubout -out secrets/snapbi-sandbox-public.pem
#
#   2. Register the PUBLIC key at Dashboard > Settings > Access Keys >
#      Payment BI SNAP, pasted whole including the BEGIN and END lines.
#      Generate in Sandbox first: doing Production first leaves the supported
#      scopes empty and needs Midtrans support to undo.
#
#   3. Midtrans then returns ClientID, ClientSecret and PartnerID, together with
#      their own public key. None of these exist in the dashboard before this
#      step, so there is nothing to look up until the exchange is done.
#
# Point at the PEM files rather than pasting them: a key is ~1700 characters and
# inline turns .env into something nobody can read or diff. Paths are relative
# to the application root. Inline MIDTRANS_SNAP_BI_PRIVATE_KEY still works for
# platforms with no writable disk.

# MIDTRANS_SNAP_BI_CLIENT_ID=
# MIDTRANS_SNAP_BI_CLIENT_SECRET=
# MIDTRANS_SNAP_BI_PARTNER_ID=
# MIDTRANS_SNAP_BI_PRIVATE_KEY_PATH=secrets/snapbi-sandbox-private.pem

# Midtrans's public key, for verifying the notifications they send you.
# This is NOT the public key you registered with them.
# MIDTRANS_SNAP_BI_PUBLIC_KEY_PATH=secrets/midtrans-snapbi-public.pem
